          "body": "This is annoying because:\n\nThe original minizip API (and thus the compatibility layer) uses various `Z_` constants defined in `zlib.h` (and `zlib-ng.h`) as possible parameters, but doesn't define them in a public header - you have to include `zlib.h` separately.\n\nThe minizip and minizip-ng libraries *do* include and build against zlib (or zlib-ng) themselves.\n\n`zlib.h` and `zlib-ng.h` are not *quite* ABI compatible - zlib-ng uses a `uint32_t` in some places where zlib uses an `unsigned int`, in structs that are exposed in the headers.\n\nSo you have to include the one that the minizip library of your choice was built against.\n\nHowever, on Windows we have to add it to the include path.\n\nBut on macOS, if we add it to the target include path as a system library, for some reason the ordering of the includes isn't respected, and it goes *before* the Qt C++ libraries, which means that the plain C standard library gets imported before the C++ standard library, resulting in badness when C++ tries to include headers that are compatible with the C standard library, as it gets the C version instead of the C++ version.",
          "author": {
            "id": 6764551,
            "username": "johnthacker",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "John Thacker",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
